  <p>
    Surveys suggest the average person has somewhere between 70 and 150 online accounts, from banks and tax portals to streaming services, shops and forums.
  </p>

  <p>
    Attacks aren't aimed only at large companies. Bots constantly try leaked passwords on ordinary people's accounts, and phishing campaigns target everyone. One compromised account can lead to others, and to identity theft or financial loss.
  </p>

  <p>
    This guide covers the main steps: strong unique passwords, multi-factor authentication (MFA), and keeping your real email address out of low-trust sites with <a href="/">disposable temporary email</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>
    1. The Foundation: Passwords and Credential Hygiene
  </h2>

  <p>
    Passwords are still the main lock on most accounts. People tend to pick predictable patterns (a capital letter first, a common word, an exclamation mark at the end), and cracking tools are built to guess exactly those.
  </p>

  <h3>
    The Biggest Mistake: Reusing Passwords
  </h3>

  <p>
    When you reuse the same password (or slight variations of it) across multiple websites, you expose yourself to <strong>credential stuffing attacks</strong>. If a gaming forum or online store you joined five years ago is breached, attackers' scripts try the same email and password on services like Amazon, PayPal, Google and Apple.
  </p>

  <h3>
    Why You Must Use a Password Manager
  </h3>

  <p>
    The only practical way to have a unique, random password of 16+ characters for every service is a reputable password manager. Well-known options include:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Bitwarden:</strong> Open source, syncs across devices, independently audited, with a free plan.</li>
    <li><strong>1Password:</strong> Paid password manager for individuals, families and businesses, protected by a separate Secret Key.</li>
    <li><strong>KeePassXC:</strong> Offline password manager that keeps your database on your own device.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    With a password manager, you only need to remember one long, memorable master passphrase (such as four randomly generated words combined with symbols). The manager creates, stores and fills in unique passwords everywhere else.
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    A reused password is only as safe as the least secure site you used it on.
  </blockquote>

  <h2>
    2. Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA): Ranking Your Defenses
  </h2>

  <p>
    Two-factor authentication (2FA) means a stolen password isn't enough on its own: an attacker also needs your phone, app or key. Some methods are stronger than others:
  </p>

  <h3>
    Good: SMS / Text Message 2FA (Better than nothing, but vulnerable)
  </h3>

  <p>
    SMS codes are much better than no 2FA, but they're vulnerable to <strong>SIM swapping attacks</strong> (where an attacker impersonates you to your cellular carrier to redirect your text messages) and interception on the phone network.
  </p>

  <h3>
    Better: Time-Based One-Time Passwords (TOTP Authenticator Apps)
  </h3>

  <p>
    Apps like Aegis Authenticator, Ente Auth, Google Authenticator or 1Password generate a new 6-digit code every 30 seconds from a secret stored on your device. Nothing is sent over the phone network, so there's nothing to intercept there.
  </p>

  <h3>
    Best: FIDO2 / WebAuthn Hardware Security Keys (Phishing-Proof)
  </h3>

  <p>
    USB or NFC security keys (such as YubiKeys) only respond to the real website's domain, so a fake login page can't use them. That makes them highly resistant to <a href="/blog/understanding-phishing-emails/">phishing attacks</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>
    3. Protect Your Email First
  </h2>

  <p>
    Your email account is the weak point for everything else. Anyone who gets into it can click "Forgot password" on your bank, Apple ID, crypto wallet and cloud storage, reset the passwords and lock you out.
  </p>

  <p>
    To protect it, <strong>keep it separate</strong>:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Lock down your primary email:</strong> Use a hardware key or an authenticator app for 2FA, a long passphrase, and never use this address on sites you don't trust.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Don't give real answers to security questions:</strong> When setting up recovery security questions ("What was your mother's maiden name?"), do not enter truthful answers that can be researched on social media. Instead, generate random passwords (e.g., <code>BlueSky#9841</code>) and store them inside your password manager under the entry's custom fields.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Use disposable emails for non-critical services:</strong> For software trials, e-book downloads, one-time discount coupons, and public forums, use <a href="/">TempoEmails</a>. Your real email then never ends up in those sites' databases.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>
    4. Proactive Account Audits & Breach Awareness
  </h2>

  <p>
    Security isn't a one-time job. Do these three checks regularly:
  </p>

  <h3>
    Check Data Breach Repositories
  </h3>

  <p>
    Visit Troy Hunt's service <a href="https://haveibeenpwned.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Have I Been Pwned</a> and enter your primary and secondary email addresses. If an account has appeared in a recent breach, immediately change the password on that service and verify that 2FA is enabled.
  </p>

  <h3>
    Audit Connected Third-Party Apps (OAuth)
  </h3>

  <p>
    When you click "Sign in with Google" or "Sign in with Apple", you grant third-party applications specific API permissions to your account. Over time, old games and apps you no longer use keep that access. Navigate to your Google / Apple Account Security settings, view all connected third-party integrations, and revoke access for any app you no longer actively use.
  </p>

  <h3>
    Review Active Sessions and Login Alerts
  </h3>

  <p>
    Most major platforms allow you to view active logged-in devices. If you see an unrecognized device or login location, use the "Log Out All Other Sessions" button and change your password immediately.
  </p>

  <h2>
    5. Where Disposable Email Helps
  </h2>

  <p>
    An effective, often overlooked step is simply having fewer places that hold your real details. Every account you create with your real email adds an entry to a database you can't control.
  </p>

  <p>
    Using <a href="/">TempoEmails</a> for low-trust signups helps:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Breaches matter less:</strong> if a site where you used a temporary address is breached, the leaked address isn't yours and doesn't lead back to you. (Still use a unique password, since the leaked password could otherwise be tried elsewhere.)
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Less spam and tracking:</strong> marketing emails go to an address you've abandoned, and that address can't be used to link you across sites. Read more about blocking trackers in our guide on <a href="/blog/what-is-email-tracking/">email tracking mechanics</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Quick verification:</strong> get the code for a one-off trial without it landing in your main inbox. Check our guide on <a href="/blog/how-to-avoid-spam/">avoiding spam emails</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>
    Your Complete Online Account Security Checklist
  </h2>

  <p>
    A checklist to work through:
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>Install and migrate all credentials to an audited password manager (Bitwarden or 1Password).</li>
    <li>Generate unique 16+ character passwords for every single active account.</li>
    <li>Enable TOTP or Hardware Key 2FA on all email, banking, social, and cloud accounts.</li>
    <li>Safely store 2FA emergency backup codes in an encrypted, offline location.</li>
    <li>Replace truthful security question answers with randomized passphrases.</li>
    <li>Check your primary email on HaveIBeenPwned and remediate compromised services.</li>
    <li>Use <a href="/">TempoEmails</a> for all low-trust, temporary, and one-off online registrations.</li>
    <li>Learn how to identify social engineering and fraudulent emails with our guide on <a href="/blog/understanding-phishing-emails/">recognizing phishing emails</a>.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    Good security doesn't mean giving up convenience. Unique passwords, multi-factor authentication and a disposable address for low-trust signups cover most of the risk. To see how TempoEmails handles your data, read the <a href="/privacy-policy/">Privacy Policy</a>.
  </p>

